Native replacements of IOP module functions (plan P5, the readable port of YYSOUND). ps2x/iop/iop_native.h: NativeContext, the interface a replacement sees (registers, IOP RAM as host memory, callGuest for imports and unported functions, jump for a tail call such as a thread body's SleepThread, charge for IOP time). IopSubsystem::registerNativeModule and registerNative: functions are registered by offset in a module's image and take effect only when the module loads with the image whose hash was registered. IopEmulator: a table checked in step() next to the import stubs; PS2X_IOP_NATIVE=0|names turns replacements off or selects them; PS2X_IOP_VERIFY=1 runs the original first (its imports happen once and are logged with the RAM they change), rolls RAM back, runs the replacement with the module's imports answered from the log, and compares RAM (the dead stack and argument home area excepted), the registers the ABI keeps, the import sequence and the return, then continues from the original's state; PS2X_IOP_DUMP_MODULES=<dir> writes the loaded image of a module with replacements. IopMemory::ramData and PS2Runtime::iopSubsystem give the port access. Blocking: a function registered with mayBlock runs on a host fiber (one per IOP thread); when guest code it called puts the thread to sleep, callFunction parks the fiber with the function's C++ frames intact, the scheduler runs other threads and, when the thread is ready again, switches back into the fiber, which carries on from where the guest code slept. The call stacks of callFunction are taken from a pool (callSlots) instead of by depth, so a parked call keeps its stack. A function that ends by jumping to an import stub (a thread body going to SleepThread or sceSifRpcLoop) gives its parkStub, which is where the verifier compares it. currentNativeContext() gives the context of the running native, kept right across nested natives and fiber switches. IopKernel::currentThread gives the running thread. Test aids of the verifier: PS2X_IOP_COVERAGE (the words of the module the original executed in verified calls), PS2X_IOP_COVERAGE_ALL (every word that runs), PS2X_IOP_PROBE (commands injected into the sound function) with PS2X_IOP_VERIFY_ALL, PS2X_IOP_TRACE_NATIVE (calls logged) and PS2X_IOP_DUMP_IMPORTS (the original's imports at a mismatch). Import effects replayed in whole words; the instructions run inside the imports are counted so that a replacement's cost excludes them; IopSubsystem::registerVerifyIgnore leaves given RAM out of the comparison after a call of a named replacement. Translated modules (plan P5.3): ps2x/iop/iop_aot.h is the interface generated code includes (AotCpu, AotContext, the interpreter's operations, aotRegistrar); IopEmulator arms a translation when the loaded image has the hash and address it was made for, runCpu runs translated code (tryAot) for as long as it stays exact (the instruction budget, the next due DMA interrupt or guest callback, no interrupt waiting; a DMA a hardware store starts is timed as the interpreter times it), PS2X_IOP_AOT=0 turns it off, PS2X_IOP_AOT_STATS counts translated and interpreted instructions per module, PS2X_IOP_VERIFY also repeats translated runs in the interpreter and compares, PS2X_IOP_TRACE_HASH logs a hash of RAM and all threads at fixed IOP times. IopMemory::ownedData, IopKernel::threads.

diff --git a/ps2xIOP/include/ps2x/iop/iop_native.h b/ps2xIOP/include/ps2x/iop/iop_native.h
new file mode 100644
--- /dev/null
+++ b/ps2xIOP/include/ps2x/iop/iop_native.h
@@ -0,0 +1,44 @@
+#pragma once
+
+#include <cstdint>
+
+namespace ps2x::iop
+{
+    // What a native (host C++) replacement of an IOP function sees and may do. The emulator calls the replacement when the IOP reaches
+    // the function's address, with the caller's registers as they are on entry (a0 to a3 are registers 4 to 7); the function ends by
+    // returning to the caller (v0, register 2, is the result) unless it calls jump().
+    class NativeContext
+    {
+    public:
+        virtual ~NativeContext() = default;
+
+        // The 2 MB of IOP RAM as host memory; an IOP address is masked to its physical part (address & 0x1FFFFF).
+        virtual uint8_t *ram() = 0;
+        virtual uint32_t reg(unsigned index) const = 0;
+        virtual void setReg(unsigned index, uint32_t value) = 0;
+
+        // Runs guest code to its return and gives v0: an import stub, or a function of the module that is not replaced. The code runs on
+        // its own stack, as the emulator's other synchronous calls do.
+        virtual uint32_t callGuest(uint32_t address, uint32_t a0 = 0, uint32_t a1 = 0, uint32_t a2 = 0, uint32_t a3 = 0) = 0;
+
+        // How many of a0 to a3 the next callGuest really passes (an import takes fewer than four: the rest are leftovers in the original, which a
+        // replacement cannot reproduce, so the verifier does not compare them).
+        virtual void argumentCount(unsigned count) = 0;
+
+        // The arguments after the fourth of the next callGuest (the caller's stack, at sp + 16, + 20, + 24 as in the o32 convention).
+        virtual void stackArguments(const uint32_t *values, unsigned count) = 0;
+
+        // Ends the function by continuing in guest code at `address` with the registers as they are now (a tail call): how a thread body
+        // sleeps (ra = where it resumes, address = the SleepThread import stub).
+        virtual void jump(uint32_t address) = 0;
+
+        // Instructions the original would have run; the IOP's time moves on by this much, so timers and DMA completions keep their schedule.
+        virtual void charge(uint32_t instructions) = 0;
+    };
+
+    using NativeFunction = void (*)(NativeContext &);
+
+    // The context of the native function that is running now (null when none). The emulator keeps it right when a function calls guest code
+    // that runs another native, and when a function that went to sleep (see IopSubsystem::registerNative, mayBlock) is resumed.
+    NativeContext *currentNativeContext() noexcept;
+}
diff --git a/ps2xIOP/include/ps2x/iop/iop_aot.h b/ps2xIOP/include/ps2x/iop/iop_aot.h
new file mode 100644
--- /dev/null
+++ b/ps2xIOP/include/ps2x/iop/iop_aot.h
@@ -0,0 +1,368 @@
+#pragma once
+
+#include <cstdint>
+#include <cstring>
+
+namespace ps2x::iop
+{
+    // Ahead-of-time translated IOP modules (plan P5.3). tools/iop_aot.py turns an IRX into C++ that runs the module's code natively and keeps
+    // the interpreter's exact behavior: one cycle per instruction, the branch and load delay slots, the traps, and the points where a run
+    // stops (the instruction budget, the next due event, an instruction that needs the emulator's per-step work). The generated code includes
+    // only this header.
+
+    // The IOP's processor state as generated code sees it: the layout of the emulator's IopCpuState (checked where the emulator hooks the
+    // translation in).
+    struct AotCpu
+    {
+        uint32_t gpr[32];
+        uint32_t hi;
+        uint32_t lo;
+        uint32_t pc;
+        uint32_t cop0[32];
+        uint32_t pendingLoadReg;
+        uint32_t pendingLoadValue;
+        bool pendingLoad;
+        bool branchPending;
+        uint32_t branchTarget;
+        bool stopped;
+        bool yielded;
+        bool exception;
+    };
+
+    inline constexpr uint32_t kAotRamSize = 2u * 1024u * 1024u;
+
+    struct AotContext
+    {
+        uint8_t *ram = nullptr;           // the 2 MB of IOP RAM
+        uint8_t *owned = nullptr;         // the per-byte ownership flags a RAM store sets (IopMemory::markOwned)
+        const uint8_t *native = nullptr;  // one byte per word of the module image: non-zero where a native replacement is installed
+        bool stop = false;                // set by an access that needs the emulator's per-step work after it (hardware, a change of the status register)
+        uint32_t hardware = 0;            // counts the accesses to hardware (the verifier cannot repeat a run that made one)
+
+        // the paths for every address that is not plain RAM (hardware, scratchpad), and the traps
+        uint32_t (*read8)(AotContext &, uint32_t address) = nullptr;
+        uint32_t (*read16)(AotContext &, uint32_t address) = nullptr;
+        uint32_t (*read32)(AotContext &, uint32_t address) = nullptr;
+        void (*write8)(AotContext &, uint32_t address, uint32_t value) = nullptr;
+        void (*write16)(AotContext &, uint32_t address, uint32_t value) = nullptr;
+        void (*write32)(AotContext &, uint32_t address, uint32_t value) = nullptr;
+        // raises the trap `code` at `faultPc` (IopCpuCore::raiseException)
+        void (*raise)(AotContext &, AotCpu &, uint32_t code, uint32_t faultPc, uint32_t delaySlot, uint32_t badAddress, uint32_t hasBadAddress) = nullptr;
+        void *impl = nullptr;
+    };
+
+    // ---- what the generated code calls: the interpreter's operations, written once
+
+    // Loads and stores: RAM directly (a store also sets the ownership flags, as IopMemory does), everything else through the context. An access
+    // that needs the emulator's per-step work after it sets context.stop, and `lim` is then zeroed so that the run ends after this instruction.
+    inline uint32_t aotLoad8(AotContext &x, uint32_t address, uint32_t &lim)
+    {
+        const uint32_t p = address & 0x1FFFFFFFu;
+        if (p < kAotRamSize)
+            return x.ram[p];
+        const uint32_t v = x.read8(x, address);
+        if (x.stop)
+            lim = 0u;
+        return v;
+    }
+
+    inline uint32_t aotLoad16(AotContext &x, uint32_t address, uint32_t &lim)
+    {
+        const uint32_t p = address & 0x1FFFFFFFu;
+        if (p + 1u < kAotRamSize)
+        {
+            uint16_t v;
+            std::memcpy(&v, x.ram + p, sizeof(v));
+            return v;
+        }
+        const uint32_t v = x.read16(x, address);
+        if (x.stop)
+            lim = 0u;
+        return v;
+    }
+
+    inline uint32_t aotLoad32(AotContext &x, uint32_t address, uint32_t &lim)
+    {
+        const uint32_t p = address & 0x1FFFFFFFu;
+        if (p + 3u < kAotRamSize)
+        {
+            uint32_t v;
+            std::memcpy(&v, x.ram + p, sizeof(v));
+            return v;
+        }
+        const uint32_t v = x.read32(x, address);
+        if (x.stop)
+            lim = 0u;
+        return v;
+    }
+
+    inline void aotStore8(AotContext &x, uint32_t address, uint32_t value, uint32_t &lim)
+    {
+        const uint32_t p = address & 0x1FFFFFFFu;
+        if (p < kAotRamSize)
+        {
+            x.ram[p] = static_cast<uint8_t>(value);
+            x.owned[p] = 1u;
+            return;
+        }
+        x.write8(x, address, value);
+        if (x.stop)
+            lim = 0u;
+    }
+
+    inline void aotStore16(AotContext &x, uint32_t address, uint32_t value, uint32_t &lim)
+    {
+        const uint32_t p = address & 0x1FFFFFFFu;
+        if (p + 1u < kAotRamSize)
+        {
+            const uint16_t v = static_cast<uint16_t>(value);
+            std::memcpy(x.ram + p, &v, sizeof(v));
+            x.owned[p] = 1u;
+            x.owned[p + 1u] = 1u;
+            return;
+        }
+        x.write16(x, address, value);
+        if (x.stop)
+            lim = 0u;
+    }
+
+    inline void aotStore32(AotContext &x, uint32_t address, uint32_t value, uint32_t &lim)
+    {
+        const uint32_t p = address & 0x1FFFFFFFu;
+        if (p + 3u < kAotRamSize)
+        {
+            std::memcpy(x.ram + p, &value, sizeof(value));
+            x.owned[p] = 1u;
+            x.owned[p + 1u] = 1u;
+            x.owned[p + 2u] = 1u;
+            x.owned[p + 3u] = 1u;
+            return;
+        }
+        x.write32(x, address, value);
+        if (x.stop)
+            lim = 0u;
+    }
+
+    // lwl, lwr, swl, swr: the unaligned halves (the memory word is the aligned one)
+    inline uint32_t aotLwl(uint32_t old, uint32_t memory, uint32_t address)
+    {
+        static constexpr uint32_t masks[4] = {0x00FFFFFFu, 0x0000FFFFu, 0x000000FFu, 0x00000000u};
+        static constexpr uint32_t shifts[4] = {24u, 16u, 8u, 0u};
+        return (old & masks[address & 3u]) | (memory << shifts[address & 3u]);
+    }
+
+    inline uint32_t aotLwr(uint32_t old, uint32_t memory, uint32_t address)
+    {
+        static constexpr uint32_t masks[4] = {0x00000000u, 0xFF000000u, 0xFFFF0000u, 0xFFFFFF00u};
+        static constexpr uint32_t shifts[4] = {0u, 8u, 16u, 24u};
+        return (old & masks[address & 3u]) | (memory >> shifts[address & 3u]);
+    }
+
+    inline uint32_t aotSwl(uint32_t old, uint32_t value, uint32_t address)
+    {
+        switch (address & 3u)
+        {
+        case 0u: return (old & 0xFFFFFF00u) | (value >> 24u);
+        case 1u: return (old & 0xFFFF0000u) | (value >> 16u);
+        case 2u: return (old & 0xFF000000u) | (value >> 8u);
+        default: return value;
+        }
+    }
+
+    inline uint32_t aotSwr(uint32_t old, uint32_t value, uint32_t address)
+    {
+        switch (address & 3u)
+        {
+        case 0u: return value;
+        case 1u: return (old & 0x000000FFu) | (value << 8u);
+        case 2u: return (old & 0x0000FFFFu) | (value << 16u);
+        default: return (old & 0x00FFFFFFu) | (value << 24u);
+        }
+    }
+
+    inline void aotMult(AotCpu &cpu, uint32_t a, uint32_t b)
+    {
+        const int64_t result = static_cast<int64_t>(static_cast<int32_t>(a)) * static_cast<int64_t>(static_cast<int32_t>(b));
+        cpu.lo = static_cast<uint32_t>(result);
+        cpu.hi = static_cast<uint32_t>(static_cast<uint64_t>(result) >> 32u);
+    }
+
+    inline void aotMultu(AotCpu &cpu, uint32_t a, uint32_t b)
+    {
+        const uint64_t result = static_cast<uint64_t>(a) * b;
+        cpu.lo = static_cast<uint32_t>(result);
+        cpu.hi = static_cast<uint32_t>(result >> 32u);
+    }
+
+    inline void aotDiv(AotCpu &cpu, uint32_t a, uint32_t b)
+    {
+        const int32_t lhs = static_cast<int32_t>(a);
+        const int32_t rhs = static_cast<int32_t>(b);
+        if (rhs == 0)
+        {
+            cpu.lo = lhs >= 0 ? 0xFFFFFFFFu : 1u;
+            cpu.hi = static_cast<uint32_t>(lhs);
+        }
+        else if (lhs == INT32_MIN && rhs == -1)
+        {
+            cpu.lo = static_cast<uint32_t>(lhs);
+            cpu.hi = 0u;
+        }
+        else
+        {
+            cpu.lo = static_cast<uint32_t>(lhs / rhs);
+            cpu.hi = static_cast<uint32_t>(lhs % rhs);
+        }
+    }
+
+    inline void aotDivu(AotCpu &cpu, uint32_t a, uint32_t b)
+    {
+        if (b == 0u)
+        {
+            cpu.lo = 0xFFFFFFFFu;
+            cpu.hi = a;
+        }
+        else
+        {
+            cpu.lo = a / b;
+            cpu.hi = a % b;
+        }
+    }
+
+    // add, addi and sub trap on signed overflow
+    inline bool aotAddOverflow(int32_t a, int32_t b, int32_t &result)
+    {
+        const int64_t wide = static_cast<int64_t>(a) + b;
+        if (wide > INT32_MAX || wide < INT32_MIN)
+            return true;
+        result = static_cast<int32_t>(wide);
+        return false;
+    }
+
+    inline bool aotSubOverflow(int32_t a, int32_t b, int32_t &result)
+    {
+        const int64_t wide = static_cast<int64_t>(a) - b;
+        if (wide > INT32_MAX || wide < INT32_MIN)
+            return true;
+        result = static_cast<int32_t>(wide);
+        return false;
+    }
+
+    // ---- the stops of a block (cold paths shared by all instructions)
+
+    // The load that is waiting to land after the instruction just executed (or none): what the next run starts with.
+    inline void aotFlushLoad(AotCpu &cpu, uint32_t loadReg, uint32_t lv)
+    {
+        if (loadReg != 0u)
+        {
+            cpu.pendingLoad = true;
+            cpu.pendingLoadReg = loadReg;
+            cpu.pendingLoadValue = lv;
+        }
+        else
+        {
+            cpu.pendingLoad = false;
+        }
+    }
+
+    // Called when the count has reached `lim` after an instruction: `dest` is the register the instruction wrote, `kind` is 0 for an ordinary one,
+    // 1 for a branch or jump (its delay slot comes next) and 2 for a delay slot, `next` the address after it, `loadReg` the register of the delayed
+    // load it started. Handles the state a run was resumed with (a load that was waiting, a branch taken before the first instruction), and
+    // returns true with the exact processor state written when the run has to end here (the budget is used up or the context says stop).
+    inline bool aotAfter(AotContext &x, AotCpu &cpu, uint32_t n, uint32_t limit, uint32_t &lim, bool &first, bool &hp, bool &bp, uint32_t pr, uint32_t pv,
+                         uint32_t bt, bool cc, uint32_t lv, uint32_t dest, uint32_t kind, uint32_t next, uint32_t loadReg)
+    {
+        if (first)
+        {
+            first = false;
+            lim = limit;
+            if (hp)
+            {
+                hp = false;
+                if (pr != dest)
+                    cpu.gpr[pr] = pv;
+            }
+            if (bp)
+            {
+                // this instruction was the delay slot of a branch taken before the run began
+                bp = false;
+                cpu.pc = bt;
+                cpu.branchPending = false;
+                cpu.exception = false;
+                aotFlushLoad(cpu, loadReg, lv);
+                return true;
+            }
+        }
+        if (n < limit && !x.stop)
+        {
+            lim = limit;
+            return false;
+        }
+        if (kind == 1u)
+        {
+            cpu.pc = next;
+            cpu.branchPending = cc;
+            cpu.branchTarget = cc ? bt : 0u;
+        }
+        else if (kind == 2u)
+        {
+            cpu.pc = cc ? bt : next;
+            cpu.branchPending = false;
+            cpu.branchTarget = cc ? bt : 0u;
+        }
+        else
+        {
+            cpu.pc = next;
+            cpu.branchPending = false;
+            cpu.branchTarget = 0u;
+        }
+        aotFlushLoad(cpu, loadReg, lv);
+        cpu.exception = false;
+        return true;
+    }
+
+    // The end of a block: the fall-through address, or the target of the branch whose delay slot was the last instruction.
+    inline uint32_t aotEnd(AotCpu &cpu, uint32_t n, bool cc, uint32_t bt, uint32_t next, uint32_t loadReg, uint32_t lv)
+    {
+        cpu.pc = cc ? bt : next;
+        cpu.branchPending = false;
+        cpu.branchTarget = cc ? bt : 0u;
+        aotFlushLoad(cpu, loadReg, lv);
+        cpu.exception = false;
+        return n;
+    }
+
+// A trap in generated code: the processor state is set by the context, and the instruction counts.
+#define AOT_TRAP(CODE, PC, DS)                                            \
+    {                                                                     \
+        x.raise(x, cpu, CODE, PC, (DS) ? 1u : 0u, 0u, 0u);                \
+        return n + 1u;                                                    \
+    }
+#define AOT_TRAP_ADDR(CODE, PC, DS, ADDRESS)                              \
+    {                                                                     \
+        x.raise(x, cpu, CODE, PC, (DS) ? 1u : 0u, (ADDRESS), 1u);         \
+        return n + 1u;                                                    \
+    }
+
+    // Runs translated code from cpu.pc until it has executed `limit` instructions, reaches code that is not translated (an import stub, a
+    // native replacement, an address outside the module) or `context.stop` is set. Returns the number of instructions executed; cpu is
+    // exactly the state the interpreter would be in after that many instructions.
+    using AotRun = uint32_t (*)(AotContext &, AotCpu &, uint32_t limit);
+
+    struct AotModule
+    {
+        const char *name;    // as the module is named when loaded (without the disc's ";1")
+        uint32_t base;       // the address the code was translated for
+        uint32_t imageSize;  // bytes of the loaded image the hash covers
+        uint64_t imageHash;  // FNV-1a over the relocated image before the module starts (as for native replacements)
+        uint32_t words;      // length of the module image in words (the size of the native mask)
+        AotRun run;
+    };
+
+    void registerAotModule(const AotModule &module);
+
+    struct AotRegistrar
+    {
+        explicit AotRegistrar(const AotModule &module) { registerAotModule(module); }
+    };
+}
